{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST supply the review prompt that each group's reviewer follows. The prompt MUST direct the reviewer not to edit files. It MUST direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text it returns, and to return design questions for the user.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has three obligations. The skill supplies the review prompt, and the prompt must direct the reviewer not to edit files. The prompt must also direct the reviewer to make mechanical changes itself in the revised text and to return design questions for the user.\n\nThe test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_makes_mechanical_changes_and_returns_design_questions]` loads the real prompt through `review_prompt()`. That helper reads `references/review-prompt.md` from the `habeas-requirements` skill. The test collapses whitespace and uses `assert_text`, which asserts `contains` for each phrase and fails with a message on any miss. It asserts three phrases:\n- \"Don't edit files; make your changes in the revised text you return\". This covers the no-edit directive.\n- \"**Mechanical changes:** make them yourself in the revised text, and never phrase them as questions\". This covers making mechanical changes in the revised text.\n- \"**Design questions:** return these; the user decides\". This covers returning design questions for the user.\n\nI confirmed that the prompt file `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md` contains each phrase. A prompt that dropped or reversed any of the three directives would fail an assertion. A prompt is text, so checking its directive wording is the appropriate test. The \"supplies\" obligation is covered because `review_prompt()` reads the skill's prompt file. I did not read `skill_file`, so I am assuming it fails when the file is missing.\n\nThe requirement has no boundary or failure cases, no ambiguity, and no `{fixme}` acknowledgments. I found no gaps.",
